% ============================================================================
% space-rock-alt-name — the everyday names NASA gives a meteor while it
% burns through the atmosphere, per the SAME already-cited sentence
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `space-rock-stage.adj`
% (`space_rock_stage(stage, description)`, the same rocky object's name at
% three different stages of its journey — meteoroid → still_a_rock_in_space,
% meteor → called_a_fireball_or_shooting_star_when_it_burns_up_in_the_
% atmosphere, meteorite → survives_the_atmosphere_and_hits_the_ground). That
% table's own header already quotes, verbatim, the meteor sentence naming
% TWO everyday alternate terms in one continuous clause — a fact the
% single-`description`-atom schema had no room to decode as two separate,
% independently queryable names:
%
%   "When meteoroids enter Earth's atmosphere ... at high speed and burn
%    up, the fireballs or "shooting stars" are called meteors."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? space_rock_alt_name(meteor, $AltName)   % fireball OR shooting_star
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `space_rock_alt_name(stage, alt_name)` carrying
% the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the alternate name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a stage the source does not give an
% alternate name for. `meteoroid` and `meteorite` are deliberately NOT rows
% here: their own already-quoted spans ("These rocks still are in space."
% and "...it's called a meteorite.") name only the ONE primary term each,
% no second everyday name, so this sibling is HONESTLY NARROWER than its
% parent — it grounds exactly the one stage whose cited span states an
% alternate term and abstains on the other two, or any other word, rather
% than inventing one. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an alternate name
% and recall which stage it names:
%
%     ? space_rock_alt_name($Stage, fireball)   % meteor
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NASA sentence
% `space-rock-stage.adj`'s own header already quotes for the meteor row —
% no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the two everyday
% synonyms that sentence's own compound clause already names, rather than
% folding them into one description atom. `trust authoritative` — the same
% tier `space-rock-stage.adj` already carries (NASA Science, a primary U.S.
% government resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table space_rock_alt_name {
    columns stage, alt_name

    row (meteor, fireball)
    row (meteor, shooting_star)

    source "When meteoroids enter Earth's atmosphere ... at high speed and burn up, the fireballs or \"shooting stars\" are called meteors."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/meteors-meteorites/"
    trust authoritative
}
